v4l2-relayd provides a GStreamer-to-v4l2loopback bridge. The packaged service
integration uses systemd. Installation does not start or enable the service.

The default settings are in /etc/default/v4l2-relayd. Version 0.2.0 uses
instances configured in /etc/v4l2-relayd.d/*.conf. Start with one instance:

    cp /etc/default/v4l2-relayd /etc/v4l2-relayd.d/camera.conf

The default VIDEOSRC="videotestsrc" produces a test pattern. To use libcamera,
build this package with USE=libcamera and change the instance configuration to:

    VIDEOSRC="libcamerasrc ! videoconvert"

For a USB camera, use its actual capture device (not the loopback output):

    VIDEOSRC="v4l2src device=/dev/video0 ! videoconvert"

Choose FORMAT, WIDTH, HEIGHT and FRAMERATE supported by the source camera.
Additional GStreamer plugins may be needed for custom pipelines, for example
JPEG decoding. USE=libcamera only installs the input plugin; it does not change
VIDEOSRC or guarantee support for a particular camera.

The packaged /etc/modprobe.d/v4l2-relayd.conf configures v4l2loopback with
exclusive_caps=1 and card_label="Virtual Camera". CARD_LABEL in the instance
configuration must match this label. The modules-load.d file loads the module
at boot. If it is not loaded yet, load it manually with:

    modprobe v4l2loopback

If the module is already loaded, changing modprobe.d does not reconfigure it.
Use your existing loopback device's label or reboot after editing the options.
For multiple instances, create separate loopback devices with distinct labels
and give each instance its corresponding CARD_LABEL.

After creating or removing instance files, regenerate systemd dependencies:

    systemctl daemon-reload
    systemctl enable --now v4l2-relayd.service

If the parent service was already running, restart it after daemon-reload.
Inspect the camera instance with:

    systemctl status v4l2-relayd@camera.service
    journalctl -u v4l2-relayd@camera.service

Open the virtual camera in a V4L2 application. The daemon uses v4l2loopback
client-usage events to activate the input camera when a consumer connects.

On systems without systemd, the executable can be started directly with the
input and output pipeline options shown in the upstream README.md. No OpenRC
init script is included.
